{
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every natural number n, including n = 0, and every function h from the finite set Omega_n of all Boolean configurations epsilon: Fin n -> Bool to R, allow arbitrary signed values, zeros and constant functions. Equip Omega_n with its canonical finite measurable structure and define mu as counting measure multiplied by the inverse of the actual cardinality of Omega_n in the extended nonnegative reals. Thus Omega_n has 2^n configurations and mu assigns mass 1/(2^n) to each configuration. For each epsilon define D(epsilon) = sum over j in Fin n of (h(epsilon) - h(epsilon with coordinate j replaced by the Boolean negation of epsilon(j)))^2. This is the full, unnormalized coordinate-flip sum, without a hidden half factor. Then mu is a probability measure; the functions epsilon -> h(epsilon)^2, epsilon -> h(epsilon)^2 log(h(epsilon)^2), and D are all Bochner integrable with respect to mu; and integral h(epsilon)^2 log(h(epsilon)^2) dmu - (integral h(epsilon)^2 dmu) log(integral h(epsilon)^2 dmu) <= (1/2) integral D(epsilon) dmu. Here log(0) = 0, zero-square logarithmic products are zero, and the mean-square integral itself, without an assumption that it equals one, is the logarithm argument and its prefactor. No hypotheses beyond n being natural and h being real-valued are supplied. The coefficient 1/2 is universal and independent of n and h. At n = 0 the carrier is the singleton empty configuration, its mass is one, D is the empty sum zero, and the entropy expression is zero, giving 0 <= 0. For every constant function h, including the zero function, D and the entropy expression are zero. Negative values of h remain admitted; coordinate-flip differences use the actual signed function values.",
  "objects": "The natural dimension n; the finite index type Fin n; the two-element Boolean type Bool with Boolean negation; the configuration carrier Omega_n=(Fin n -> Bool); an arbitrary real-valued function h on Omega_n; actual cardinality of Omega_n; counting measure; locally defined normalized measure mu; single-coordinate updates of each configuration; locally defined full-flip sum D; three integrand functions; and their Bochner integrals.",
  "domains": "n ranges over N including zero. Fin n consists of indices strictly less than n and is empty at n=0. Configurations are all functions from Fin n to Bool with the canonical finite measurable structure. h takes values in R with no sign restriction. The measure scaling factor is the cardinality inverse in the extended nonnegative reals. D, square/logarithm expressions, entropy, coefficient 1/2 and the final order comparison are real-valued. Log arguments are nonnegative squares or the nonnegative mean square, with zero admitted.",
  "quantifiers": "Universally for every n in N and then every function h:Omega_n -> R, define mu and D locally and conclude the entire conjunction. In defining D, each epsilon ranges over Omega_n and the finite sum ranges over every j in Fin n. Integrals range over the full configuration carrier under this same mu. There are no existential constants or supplied certificates.",
  "assumptions": "Only the types of n and h and the approved canonical finite measurable structure are fixed. h is arbitrary, signed, possibly zero or constant. There are no supplied probability, integrability, entropy, regularity, smoothness, domain, independence, law or inequality assumptions. Probability status of mu and each stated integrability assertion are conclusions. The log(0)=0 convention and Boolean update semantics are definitions.",
  "conclusion": "The conjunction asserts total mass mu(Omega_n)=1; Bochner integrability of h^2 under mu; Bochner integrability of h^2 log(h^2) under mu; Bochner integrability of D under mu; and the non-strict inequality integral h^2 log(h^2) dmu - (integral h^2 dmu) log(integral h^2 dmu) <= (1/2) integral D dmu. The left side uses the actual mean square and does not require h^2 to be a normalized density.",
  "scopes": "mu is the one locally defined measure used in the probability assertion, all three integrability assertions, and every integral. D is the one locally defined sum over all coordinates. Function.update flips exactly coordinate j using the old Boolean value epsilon(j) and leaves every other coordinate unchanged. The sum has no 1/2; the sole final prefactor is explicitly outside its integral. Both occurrences of the mean-square integral on the left are identical. The quantifier includes n=0, empty-coordinate sums, zero logarithm arguments, all constants and all signed real functions.",
  "constant_dependencies": "The fixed coefficient 1/2 in the inequality has no dependence on n or h. The probability-measure scaling is exactly the inverse of the actual configuration cardinality 2^n and depends only on n. There are no dimension-dependent inequality constants, bounds on h, chosen normalizations or hidden parameters.",
